(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["anatomy-exam-4-appendicular-musculature-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"The Appendicular Musculature — Anatomy Exam 4 — Set 2","category":"Anatomy","exam":"Exam 4","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"How are the muscles of the lower limbs described in comparison to the upper limbs, and into what three groups are they divided?","choices":["Larger and more powerful; divided into muscles that move the thigh, muscles that move the leg, and muscles that move the foot and toes","Larger and more powerful than the upper limb muscles, but divided instead into axial and appendicular groups based on skeletal attachment","Smaller and weaker than the upper limb muscles, and divided into flexor, extensor, and rotator groups based purely on the action produced at each joint","Roughly equal in size and power to the upper limb muscles, and divided simply into intrinsic and extrinsic groups based on their origin in the foot"],"answer":0,"exp":"The muscles of the lower limbs are larger and more powerful than those of the upper limbs, and are divided into three groups: muscles that move the thigh, muscles that move the leg, and muscles that move the foot and toes. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"Into what four groups are the muscles that move the thigh divided?","choices":["Flexor group, extensor group, abductor group, and adductor group","Anterior group, posterior group, medial group, and lateral group","Superficial group, intermediate group, deep group, and reflected group","Gluteal group, lateral rotator group, adductor group, and iliopsoas group"],"answer":3,"exp":"Muscles that move the thigh originate on the pelvis and are divided into four groups: the gluteal group, lateral rotator group, adductor group, and iliopsoas group. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"Which muscle of the gluteal group produces posterior extension at the hip and inserts on the iliotibial tract and gluteal tuberosity of the femur?","choices":["Gluteus medius","Gluteus maximus","Tensor fasciae latae","Gluteus minimus"],"answer":1,"exp":"Gluteus maximus produces posterior extension at the hip, originating from the iliac crest to the sacrum and inserting on the iliotibial tract and gluteal tuberosity of the femur. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Which two gluteal group muscles both produce abduction at the hip, alongside tensor fasciae latae?","choices":["Piriformis and quadratus femoris","Gluteus medius and gluteus minimus","Adductor longus and adductor brevis","Gluteus maximus and gluteus medius"],"answer":1,"exp":"Gluteus medius and gluteus minimus both produce abduction at the hip, the same action as tensor fasciae latae. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Which lateral rotator group muscle produces only lateral rotation of the hip, without an abduction component (unlike the other three muscles in its group)?","choices":["Gemelli muscles","Piriformis muscle","Obturator muscles","Quadratus femoris"],"answer":3,"exp":"Quadratus femoris produces lateral rotation of the hip only, distinguishing it from the obturators, piriformis, and gemelli, which each produce both lateral rotation and abduction of the hip. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"Which three muscles converge and insert at the medial tibial tubercle to form the pes anserine bursae?","choices":["Gracilis, sartorius, and semitendinosus","Pectineus, gracilis, and adductor magnus","Vastus medialis, vastus lateralis, and rectus femoris","Adductor longus, adductor brevis, and adductor magnus"],"answer":0,"exp":"Gracilis, sartorius, and semitendinosus converge and insert at the medial tibial tubercle to form the pes anserine bursae. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"Which adductor group muscle produces both flexion and medial rotation at the knee, as well as adduction and medial rotation at the hip?","choices":["Adductor brevis","Pectineus","Adductor magnus","Gracilis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Gracilis produces flexion and medial rotation at the knee, as well as adduction and medial rotation at the hip. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"Which two muscles make up the iliopsoas group, and what is their shared action?","choices":["Psoas major and gracilis; both flex at the knee","Iliacus and sartorius; both flex at the hip and knee","Iliacus and pectineus; both adduct at the hip","Iliacus and psoas major; both flex at the hip"],"answer":3,"exp":"The iliopsoas group consists of the iliacus (flexion at the hip) and psoas major, noted as a major flexor of the hip. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"What four muscles are collectively called the quadriceps femoris?","choices":["Rectus femoris, biceps femoris, semimembranosus, and semitendinosus","Vastus intermedius, vastus lateralis, vastus medialis, and sartorius","Vastus lateralis, vastus medialis, gracilis, and rectus femoris","Vastus intermedius, vastus lateralis, vastus medialis, and rectus femoris"],"answer":3,"exp":"The three vastus muscles (intermedius, lateralis, medialis) and the rectus femoris muscle are collectively called the quadriceps femoris. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"Which quadriceps muscle also produces flexion at the hip, in addition to extending the lower leg?","choices":["Rectus femoris","Vastus intermedius","Vastus medialis","Vastus lateralis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Rectus femoris extends the lower leg and also produces flexion at the hip, since it crosses both the hip and knee joints. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"Which three muscles are collectively called the hamstrings?","choices":["Semimembranosus, semitendinosus, and gracilis","Biceps femoris, sartorius, and popliteus","Popliteus, sartorius, and rectus femoris","Biceps femoris, semimembranosus, and semitendinosus"],"answer":3,"exp":"The biceps femoris, semimembranosus, and semitendinosus are collectively called the hamstrings. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"Which muscle allows crossing of the lower leg (as in sitting cross-legged) and flexes both the knee and the hip?","choices":["Popliteus","Semimembranosus","Biceps femoris","Sartorius"],"answer":3,"exp":"Sartorius allows crossing of the lower leg and flexes both the knee and hip. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"Which hamstring muscle also produces extension at the hip, in addition to flexing the lower leg?","choices":["Biceps femoris","Semitendinosus","Popliteus","Semimembranosus"],"answer":0,"exp":"Biceps femoris flexes the lower leg and also produces extension at the hip. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"What distinguishes extrinsic muscles of the foot and toes from intrinsic muscles of the toes?","choices":["Extrinsic muscles originate on the lower leg but move the foot and toes; intrinsic muscles originate on the foot itself but move the toes","Both extrinsic and intrinsic muscles originate on the lower leg and there is no meaningful distinction in their origin, insertion, or function","Extrinsic muscles originate on the foot itself but move the toes; intrinsic muscles originate on the lower leg but move the foot and toes","Extrinsic muscles move only the toes and never the ankle; intrinsic muscles move only the ankle and never the toes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Extrinsic muscles of the foot and toes originate on the lower leg but move the foot and toes, while intrinsic muscles of the toes originate on some aspect of the foot itself but move the toes. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"Which muscle dorsiflexes the foot, originating on the proximal tibia and inserting on the medial cuneiform and 1st metatarsal base?","choices":["Tibialis posterior","Tibialis anterior","Gastrocnemius","Soleus"],"answer":1,"exp":"Tibialis anterior dorsiflexes the foot, originating on the proximal tibia and inserting on the medial cuneiform and base of the 1st metatarsal, innervated by the peroneal nerve. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"Which two muscles produce eversion of the foot?","choices":["Fibularis brevis and fibularis longus","Flexor digitorum longus and flexor hallucis longus","Gastrocnemius and soleus","Tibialis anterior and tibialis posterior"],"answer":0,"exp":"Fibularis brevis and fibularis longus both produce eversion of the foot. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"Which large posterior leg muscle produces plantar flexion and is notable for having its tendon become the calcaneal (Achilles) tendon along with the gastrocnemius?","choices":["Tibialis anterior","Fibularis brevis","Soleus","Extensor digitorum longus"],"answer":2,"exp":"Soleus produces plantar flexion of the foot, and together with the gastrocnemius, contributes to the calcaneal tendon. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"Which muscle produces plantar flexion and inversion of the foot, running deep in the posterior leg?","choices":["Tibialis posterior","Tibialis anterior","Extensor hallucis longus","Fibularis longus"],"answer":0,"exp":"Tibialis posterior produces plantar flexion and inversion of the foot. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"Which extrinsic muscle produces flexion of toes 2 through 5?","choices":["Flexor hallucis longus","Flexor digitorum longus","Extensor hallucis longus","Extensor digitorum longus"],"answer":1,"exp":"Flexor digitorum longus produces flexion of toes 2 through 5. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"Which muscle produces flexion of the hallux (great toe) specifically, as an extrinsic muscle of the foot?","choices":["Extensor hallucis longus","Flexor hallucis longus","Flexor digitorum longus","Abductor hallucis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Flexor hallucis longus produces flexion of the hallux. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"Which intrinsic foot muscle abducts the hallux (great toe)?","choices":["Extensor digitorum brevis","Flexor hallucis brevis","Adductor hallucis","Abductor hallucis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Abductor hallucis produces abduction of the hallux. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 83"},{"q":"Which intrinsic foot muscle produces flexion of toes 2 through 5 and is located in the sole of the foot?","choices":["Quadratus plantae","Flexor digitorum brevis","Extensor digitorum brevis","Flexor digitorum longus"],"answer":1,"exp":"Flexor digitorum brevis is an intrinsic muscle that produces flexion of toes 2 through 5. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 83"},{"q":"What is the specific function of quadratus plantae in the foot?","choices":["Flexion of toes 2 through 5, assisting the flexor digitorum longus tendons","Abduction of the little toe away from the midline of the foot","Extension of toes 2 through 5 at the metatarsophalangeal joints","Adduction of the great toe toward the second toe, opposite abduction"],"answer":0,"exp":"Quadratus plantae produces flexion of toes 2 through 5. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 85"},{"q":"In the foot, which intrinsic muscles produce extension of toes 2 through 5 — notably the opposite action from their same-named counterparts in the hand?","choices":["Abductor digiti minimi","Lumbricals","Quadratus plantae","Flexor digitorum brevis"],"answer":1,"exp":"The lumbricals of the foot produce extension of toes 2 through 5, in contrast to the lumbricals of the hand, which produce flexion of the digits. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 85"},{"q":"What is the function of the deep fasciae separating limb muscles into compartments?","choices":["They are the primary structures responsible for transmitting electrical nerve signals directly into the muscle fibers","They form the dense fibrous tendons that anchor the ends of each skeletal muscle directly to the periosteum covering nearby bone","They form the periosteum, the fibrous membrane that covers and nourishes the surface of the underlying limb bones","They support and interconnect adjacent skeletal muscles while permitting independent movement, separating soft tissues into compartments"],"answer":3,"exp":"The connective tissue fibers of the deep fasciae support and interconnect adjacent skeletal muscles but permit independent movement, and the deep fascia separates the soft tissues of the limb into separate compartments. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 90"},{"q":"Which three muscles make up the anterior compartment of the arm?","choices":["Triceps brachii, biceps brachii, and brachialis","Biceps brachii, brachioradialis, and supinator","Triceps brachii alone, the posterior compartment muscle","Biceps brachii, brachialis, and coracobrachialis"],"answer":3,"exp":"The anterior compartment of the arm contains the biceps brachii, brachialis, and coracobrachialis. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 92"},{"q":"Which muscle makes up the posterior compartment of the arm?","choices":["Biceps brachii","Brachialis","Triceps brachii","Coracobrachialis"],"answer":2,"exp":"The posterior compartment of the arm is made up of the triceps brachii. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 92"},{"q":"Into what three compartments is the thigh divided?","choices":["Superficial and deep compartments only","Flexor and extensor compartments only","Anterior (extensor), medial (adductor), and posterior (flexor) compartments","Anterior, lateral, and posterior compartments only, with no medial compartment"],"answer":2,"exp":"The thigh is divided into the anterior compartment (extensor compartment), medial compartment (adductor compartment), and posterior compartment (flexor compartment). — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 103"},{"q":"Which muscle group makes up the medial (adductor) compartment of the thigh?","choices":["The hamstrings -- biceps femoris, semimembranosus, and semitendinosus -- of the posterior thigh compartment","Iliopsoas, quadriceps femoris, and sartorius, the muscles of the anterior thigh compartment","Gluteus maximus and the lateral rotator group of the posterior hip region","Pectineus, adductor brevis, adductor longus, adductor magnus, gracilis, and obturator externus"],"answer":3,"exp":"The medial (adductor) compartment of the thigh contains the pectineus, adductor brevis, adductor longus, adductor magnus, gracilis, and obturator externus. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 107"},{"q":"Which muscle group makes up the posterior (flexor) compartment of the thigh?","choices":["The hamstrings","The adductor group","The iliopsoas group","The quadriceps femoris"],"answer":0,"exp":"The posterior (flexor) compartment of the thigh is made up of the hamstrings. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 110"}]};
